lesson: 167
title: Chickens and rabbits in disguise: wheels, tickets, boats
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Any story with two kinds of things, a total count and a total of something else, is a chickens and rabbits problem.
explain: 12 bicycles and tricycles have 29 wheels. If all were bicycles, there would be 24 wheels; 5 wheels are left over, and each tricycle has 1 wheel more, so there are 5 tricycles.
explain: Tickets work the same way: if all were cheap tickets, the money missing shows how many dear ones there are; divide it by the difference in price.
vars: a=name x=2..20 y=1..20 p=2..9 e=1..6 w=1..3
let: q = p + e
let: n = x + y
need: q <= 12
need: q * y > w * x
answer: y
exercise: In a bicycle shed there are bicycles and tricycles, {n} in all, with {2*x+3*y} wheels. How many tricycles are there?
exercise: A class bought {n} tickets to a show. A child ticket costs {p} dollars and an adult ticket costs {q} dollars. They paid {p*x+q*y} dollars. How many adult tickets did they buy?
exercise: There are spiders and beetles in a box: {n} of them with {8*x+6*y} legs. A spider has 8 legs and a beetle has 6. How many beetles are in the box?
exercise: {p*x+q*y} children rowed across a lake in {n} boats, and every boat was full. A big boat takes {q} children and a small one takes {p}. How many big boats were there?
exercise: {a} bought {n} stamps, some at {p} cents and the rest at {q} cents. {a.He} paid {p*x+q*y} cents. How many {q}-cent stamps did {a.he} buy?
exercise: Cars and motorbikes stand in a car park: {n} vehicles with {2*x+4*y} wheels. How many cars are there?
test: How many tricycles are there if a playground has {n} bicycles and tricycles with {2*x+3*y} wheels altogether?
test: A museum sold {n} tickets for {p*x+q*y} dollars. Cheap tickets cost {p} dollars and dear ones {q} dollars. How many dear tickets were sold?
test: A quiz has {n} questions. A right answer gives {q} points and a wrong answer takes away {w} points. {a} answered every question and scored {q*y-w*x} points. How many did {a.he} get right?
test: There are {8*x+6*y} legs on the spiders and beetles in a jar, and {n} creatures in all. Spiders have eight legs, beetles six. How many are beetles?
exercise: A shop in Ohio sells bicycles and tricycles. It has {n} of them, with {2*x+3*y} wheels in total. How many tricycles does it have?
exercise: How many adult tickets were sold if {n} tickets to a museum in London brought in {p*x+q*y} pounds, a child ticket costing {p} pounds and an adult ticket {q} pounds?
exercise: {a} has {n} coins, some worth {p} cents and the others worth {q} cents. Together they make {p*x+q*y} cents. How many {q}-cent coins does {a.he} have?
exercise: A parking lot in Texas has cars and motorcycles: {n} vehicles and {2*x+4*y} wheels altogether. How many cars are there?
exercise: In a jar there are {n} bugs: spiders with 8 legs and beetles with 6 legs. The bugs have {8*x+6*y} legs. How many of them are beetles?
exercise: A school in Sydney took {p*x+q*y} pupils to the beach in {n} minibuses, all of them full. A big minibus seats {q} pupils and a small one seats {p}. How many big minibuses went?
exercise: {a} bought {n} pens for {p*x+q*y} dollars. Some cost {p} dollars each and the rest cost {q} dollars each. How many {q}-dollar pens did {a.he} buy?
exercise: At a playground in Ireland there are {2*x+3*y} wheels on {n} bikes and trikes. How many trikes are there?
test: A school play in Toronto sold {n} tickets: student tickets at {p} dollars and adult tickets at {q} dollars. The takings were {p*x+q*y} dollars. Find the number of adult tickets.
test: In a quiz in Dublin every right answer earns {q} points and every wrong one loses {w} points. {a} answered all {n} questions and finished with {q*y-w*x} points. How many answers were right?
test: {n} vehicles stand in a garage in Wales, cars and motorbikes, and their wheels number {2*x+4*y}. How many are cars?
test: How many beetles are there among {n} spiders and beetles that have {8*x+6*y} legs altogether, if a spider has eight legs and a beetle six?
test: {p*x+q*y} campers crossed a river in New Zealand in {n} canoes, every canoe full. Large canoes hold {q} campers and small ones {p}. How many large canoes were used?
test: A cycle hire shop has {n} bikes and trikes for rent. Counting the wheels gives {2*x+3*y}. How many trikes are there for rent?
exercise: bikes n trikes in the shed, {n} of em, {2*x+3*y} wheels. how many trikes
exercise: class got {n} tickets, kids {p} dollar, adults {q}, paid {p*x+q*y+1} — no wait, {p*x+q*y} dollars. how many adult tickets
exercise: Sam says all {n} are bikes so 0 trikes lol. nah, {2*x+3*y} wheels on {n} bikes n trikes, how many trikes
exercise: spiders n beetles in a box, {n} of em, {8*x+6*y} legs (spider's 8, beetle's 6), {a} found em in the shed. how many beetles
test: {n} tickets sold, cheap ones {p} dollar, dear ones {q}, took {p*x+q*y} dollars. how many dear ones
test: {n} wheelies in the shed, bikes n trikes, {2*x+3*y} wheels, my nan's got 2 bikes btw. trikes?
test: {a} reckons all {n} must be trikes. nah, {2*x+3*y} wheels on {n} bikes n trikes — how many trikes really
exercise: After Gran passed away, the family bought {n} train tickets to the funeral. A child ticket cost {p} dollars and an adult ticket {q} dollars, {p*x+q*y} dollars in all. How many adult tickets did they buy?
exercise: dad got let go and we're skint, and he STILL paid {p*x+q*y} dollars for {n} tickets to the footy. kids {p}, adults {q}. how many adult ones, genius
test: {a}'s uncle was economical with the truth about his shed: he said it held only bicycles, but there are bicycles and tricycles, {n} in all, with {2*x+3*y} wheels. How many tricycles are in it?
exam: It is given that a shed contains bicycles and tricycles, {n} in number, with {2*x+3*y} wheels in aggregate. Determine the number of tricycles.
exam: Whereas a class purchased {n} tickets, a child's ticket costing {p} dollars and an adult's ticket {q} dollars, and the total expenditure incurred amounted to {p*x+q*y} dollars, ascertain the number of adult tickets purchased.
exam: Ascertain the number of three-wheeled vehicles in a yard containing exclusively two-wheeled and three-wheeled vehicles, {n} vehicles in all, having {2*x+3*y} wheels collectively.
exam: A stationer sold {n} notebooks in a single day, some at {p} dollars apiece and the remainder at {q} dollars apiece, receiving {p*x+q*y} dollars in total. State the number of notebooks sold at the higher price.
